.TH XMEMORY 3 "2026" "Extern Library" "Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
xmalloc, xcalloc, xrealloc, xfree, xmemset, xmemcpy, xmemccpy, xmemmove, xmemcmp, xmempcpy - memory allocation and memory manipulation utilities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "xmemory.h"

XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xmalloc(size_t size);
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xcalloc(size_t nmemb, size_t size);
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xrealloc(void *ptr, size_t size);
XSTDAPI void XCALLAPI xfree(void *ptr);

XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xmemset(void *s, int c, size_t n);
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xmemcpy(void *xrestrict dest, const void *xrestrict src, size_t n);
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xmemccpy(void *xrestrict dest, const void *xrestrict src, int c, size_t n);
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xmemmove(void *dest, const void *src, size_t n);
XSTDAPI int XCALLAPI xmemcmp(const void *s1, const void *s2, size_t n);
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xmempcpy(void *xrestrict dest, const void *xrestrict src, size_t n);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B xmemory
interface provides lightweight wrappers around memory allocation and
memory manipulation functions used by the Extern Library.

The interface provides a consistent Extern Library naming and ABI layer
while preserving the semantics of the underlying C memory functions.

The API includes dynamic allocation, zero-initialized allocation,
reallocation, deallocation, memory initialization, copying, moving,
comparison, and copy-until-character operations.
.SH HEADER
The public interface is provided by:
.nf
#include "xmemory.h"
.fi
The header includes:
.nf
#include "xstddef.h"
.fi
.SH FUNCTIONS
.SS xmalloc()
.nf
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xmalloc(size_t size);
.fi
Allocates
.I size
bytes using
.BR malloc (3).
The returned memory is uninitialized.
.SS xcalloc()
.nf
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xcalloc(size_t nmemb, size_t size);
.fi
Allocates storage for
.I nmemb
objects of
.I size
bytes each using
.BR calloc (3).
The allocated storage is initialized according to
.BR calloc (3)
semantics.
.SS xrealloc()
.nf
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xrealloc(void *ptr, size_t size);
.fi
Changes the size of an existing allocation using
.BR realloc (3).
Behavior follows the underlying implementation.
.SS xfree()
.nf
XSTDAPI void XCALLAPI xfree(void *ptr);
.fi
Releases memory using
.BR free (3).
Passing NULL follows the semantics of
.BR free (3).
.SS xmemset()
.nf
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xmemset(void *s, int c, size_t n);
.fi
Fills the first
.I n
bytes of the object pointed to by
.I s
with the byte value represented by
.I c .
This is a wrapper around
.BR memset (3)
and returns the destination pointer.
.SS xmemcpy()
.nf
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xmemcpy(void *xrestrict dest, const void *xrestrict src, size_t n);
.fi
Copies
.I n
bytes from
.I src
to
.I dest
using
.BR memcpy (3).
The source and destination regions must not overlap.
The function returns
.I dest .
.SS xmemccpy()
.nf
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xmemccpy(void *xrestrict dest, const void *xrestrict src, int c, size_t n);
.fi
Copies up to
.I n
bytes from
.I src
.to
.I dest
using
.BR memccpy (3).
Copying stops after the first byte matching the value represented by
.I c .
The return value follows the underlying
.BR memccpy (3)
implementation.
.B memccpy
is not universally provided by ISO C implementations; availability
depends on the target platform and portability layer.
.SS xmemmove()
.nf
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xmemmove(void *dest, const void *src, size_t n);
.fi
Copies
.I n
bytes from
.I src
.to
.I dest
using
.BR memmove (3).
Unlike
.BR xmemcpy ,
overlapping source and destination regions are supported.
The function returns
.I dest .
.SS xmemcmp()
.nf
XSTDAPI int XCALLAPI xmemcmp(const void *s1, const void *s2, size_t n);
.fi
Compares the first
.I n
bytes of
.I s1
and
.I s2
using
.BR memcmp (3).
The return value follows the comparison semantics of
.BR memcmp (3).
.SS xmempcpy()
.nf
XSTDAPI void *XCALLAPI xmempcpy(void *xrestrict dest, const void *xrestrict src, size_t n);
.fi
Copies
.I n
bytes from
.I src
.to
.I dest
using
.BR mempcpy (3).
The function returns a pointer to the byte immediately following the
copied destination range.
.B mempcpy
is not part of ISO C on all platforms.
.SH C++ COMPATIBILITY
The exported functions are enclosed by:
.nf
#ifdef __cplusplus
extern "C" {
#endif
.fi
and:
.nf
#ifdef __cplusplus
}
#endif
.fi
This provides C linkage when the header is included from C++.
.SH ABI AND EXPORT MACROS
The public functions use the Extern Library ABI macros:
.nf
XSTDAPI
XCALLAPI
.fi
These macros may control symbol visibility, DLL import/export behavior,
calling conventions, and other platform-specific ABI properties.
.SH SEMANTICS
The XMEMORY functions are thin wrappers and do not add bounds checking,
ownership tracking, lifetime management, or synchronization.
Callers remain responsible for satisfying the requirements of the
underlying C functions.
.PP
In particular:
.IP \(bu 2
.B xmalloc()
returns uninitialized storage.
.IP \(bu 2
.B xcalloc()
follows
.BR calloc (3)
initialization semantics.
.IP \(bu 2
.B xrealloc()
follows
.BR realloc (3)
semantics.
.IP \(bu 2
.B xfree()
must be used with a compatible allocated pointer or NULL.
.IP \(bu 2
.B xmemcpy()
requires non-overlapping source and destination regions.
.IP \(bu 2
.B xmemmove()
supports overlapping regions.
.IP \(bu 2
Memory operations require valid accessible memory ranges.
.IP \(bu 2
.B xmemcmp()
requires both compared ranges to be readable for the requested length.
.IP \(bu 2
.B xmemccpy()
and
.B xmempcpy()
depend on platform support for their underlying functions.
.SH RETURN VALUES
.TS
box tab(@);
l l.
Function@Return value
_
xmalloc()@Pointer returned by malloc()
xcalloc()@Pointer returned by calloc()
xrealloc()@Pointer returned by realloc()
xfree()@No return value
xmemset()@Destination pointer
xmemcpy()@Destination pointer
xmemccpy()@Return value of memccpy()
xmemmove()@Destination pointer
xmemcmp()@Comparison result from memcmp()
xmempcpy()@Return value of mempcpy()
.TE
.SH ERRORS
The XMEMORY interface does not define a separate error-reporting
mechanism.
.PP
Allocation failures are reported through the return values of
.BR xmalloc (),
.BR xcalloc (),
and
.BR xrealloc ().
.PP
Invalid pointers, invalid ranges, prohibited overlap, and other
violations of the underlying function contracts are not converted into
XMEMORY-specific error codes.
.SH THREAD SAFETY
The wrappers do not maintain library-level mutable state.
Thread-safety characteristics therefore follow those of the underlying
allocation and memory functions supplied by the target C runtime and
platform.
.PP
Applications remain responsible for synchronizing access to shared
memory objects when required.
.SH PORTABILITY
The standard memory-operation wrappers inherit the portability
characteristics of their underlying C facilities.
.PP
.B memccpy()
and
.B mempcpy()
are not universally provided by ISO C implementations. A platform
portability implementation may therefore be required where those
functions are unavailable.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include "xmemory.h"

int main(void)
{
    int *values = xcalloc(16, sizeof(*values));

    if (values == NULL)
        return 1;

    values[0] = 42;

    int *tmp = xrealloc(values, 32 * sizeof(*values));

    if (tmp == NULL) {
        xfree(values);
        return 1;
    }

    values = tmp;
    xfree(values);

    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH HEADER GUARD
The header uses:
.nf
#ifndef __XMEMORY_H__
#define __XMEMORY_H__
.fi
The supplied implementation currently ends its comment with
.B __XSTRING_H__ .
The guard name is
.B __XMEMORY_H__ ;
the closing comment should normally be:
.nf
#endif /* __XMEMORY_H__ */
.fi
